ally favor the  white cam  so obviously we are not going to fall  for it  rather we have the stunning reply here  as well  starting with knight e captures g4  bam  well what's the point what happens if  white take our piece  the answer is very simple knight  captures e4  not only attacking the rook but in the  next turn  we are going to take on e7 and again  black emerge with a material advantage  for example one of the game from the  database  continue with rook d2h2  chop up this knight and now white played  edge captures g6 which at first sight  looks very threatening but the black  player  who was the international master  continue  with this very accurate sequence  knight to g3 attacking two pieces  white gives some checks first  rook check then pawn check  but after rook captures 7 he realized  that  his rook is hanging as well as the  bishop  so he continued with bishop to c4  pinning down the black rogue but now  black  delivered the final blow knight captures  even  rook captures avon and now rook captures  e3  forcing white to resign the game as  clearly  there is no compensation for the loss of  the extra piece  last but not least what happens if you  opponent continue with  g5 which is in fact one of the best  reply  and against this my recommendation is  you should continue with this  very flashy move knight captures e4  once again white response is force he  has to take this knight  and after that we are going to take on  e7  and if you count the material at this  point yes  indeed it is balanced but the  big time problem arise in the near  future  as white e4 pawn is most likely a goner  and as i have highlighted over here  black has a very simple plan  to gobble up that weak bone  now to understand this point i like to  show you one of the game from the  database  where black finished the white very  quickly  white responded here with knight to b3  attacking on d6  and accordingly black counter-attack  with  bishop to c6  okay why defend it with bish